<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  What Android is allowed to carry off this device, for API 31+.

  The app is a one-way archiver: the mailbox holds the chats and is safe
  wherever the phone ends up. What lives only here is the record of what has
  already been sent, and losing that does not lose anything - it makes the app
  mail every chat a second time, into a mailbox with no way to tell the copies
  apart. That record is sync_state.db, and it is the whole reason this file
  exists.

  Allow-list, not a deny-list: naming any <include> switches the whole domain
  to opt-in, so anything not listed here is excluded by construction rather
  than by remembering to exclude it. That matters most for data/inbox and
  data/processed, which hold the user's actual chat exports - hundreds of
  megabytes of message text that has no business leaving the device, and that
  would blow past Auto Backup's 25 MB quota anyway.

  sync_state.db without its -wal: in WAL mode the main file is always a valid
  database on its own, merely a little behind. Restoring a -wal taken at a
  different instant than the -db it belongs to is the one way to end up with a
  file that will not open at all, and "a few messages sent twice" is a far
  cheaper failure than "the record is unreadable". They are excluded by
  omission; the exclude entries below are there to say so out loud.

  chatmailsync_prefs also carries the Keystore-encrypted mail password. The
  ciphertext travels; the key does not - Keystore key material is
  non-exportable and never leaves the device it was generated on. On the
  restored device SecretStore.getSecret() fails its GCM tag check, clears the
  dead blob and sets imap_password_lost, which is the path the app already
  takes after a factory-reset-protection event. The user enters the password
  once, and it is the only thing they have to re-enter.

  What a restore therefore exposes to whoever holds the backup: chat display
  names, one-way message hashes, run history, the mail address, the IMAP host
  and the watched-folder path. Not message text, and not the password. On
  modern devices Auto Backup is end-to-end encrypted with a key derived from
  the lock-screen secret, so Google cannot read it either.
-->
<data-extraction-rules>
    <cloud-backup>
        <include domain="file" path="chatmailsync/data/sync_state.db" />
        <include domain="sharedpref" path="chatmailsync_prefs.xml" />
        <exclude domain="file" path="chatmailsync/data/sync_state.db-wal" />
        <exclude domain="file" path="chatmailsync/data/sync_state.db-shm" />
    </cloud-backup>
    <!-- Smart Switch and the other cable/Wi-Fi device-to-device transfers.
         Same set: nothing here is worth carrying that is not worth backing
         up, and a phone-to-phone move is the case the manual bundle was
         written for, so the two must agree about what a move preserves. -->
    <device-transfer>
        <include domain="file" path="chatmailsync/data/sync_state.db" />
        <include domain="sharedpref" path="chatmailsync_prefs.xml" />
        <exclude domain="file" path="chatmailsync/data/sync_state.db-wal" />
        <exclude domain="file" path="chatmailsync/data/sync_state.db-shm" />
    </device-transfer>
</data-extraction-rules>
